00:01:56.150 --> 00:01:58.760
OK, long intro, I know, but

00:01:58.790 --> 00:01:59.950
thanks for bearing with me,

00:02:00.260 --> 00:02:02.260
and let's get right into it.

00:02:03.660 --> 00:02:09.670
On the morning of 13 October 1972, a

00:02:09.680 --> 00:02:12.480
group of young men boarded a Uruguayan

00:02:12.540 --> 00:02:14.859
Air Force plane in Montevideo.

00:02:15.880 --> 00:02:18.160
Most of them were members of the

00:02:18.200 --> 00:02:21.070
Old Christians Club rugby team, a

00:02:21.070 --> 00:02:23.709
team made up of former students of

00:02:23.710 --> 00:02:26.130
a Catholic school in a well-to-do

00:02:26.150 --> 00:02:28.479
neighbourhood of the Uruguayan capital.

00:02:29.219 --> 00:02:32.359
They were young, between 18 and 25,

00:02:32.860 --> 00:02:34.830
they were healthy, and they were looking

00:02:34.830 --> 00:02:37.760
forward to a rugby tour of Santiago,

00:02:37.940 --> 00:02:40.870
Chile, followed by a few days of holiday.

00:02:41.969 --> 00:02:45.000
With the players came friends, family

00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:47.289
members, and a handful of other

00:02:47.300 --> 00:02:49.829
passengers with no connection to the team.

00:02:50.460 --> 00:02:52.020
45 people in total.

00:02:52.670 --> 00:02:54.720
It was, by any measure,

00:02:55.040 --> 00:02:56.700
an unremarkable flight.

00:02:57.480 --> 00:03:00.340
Except, of course, that it wasn't.

00:03:01.440 --> 00:03:05.000
To fly from Montevideo to Santiago, you

00:03:05.000 --> 00:03:07.500
have to cross the Andes mountain range.

00:03:07.970 --> 00:03:10.500
And crossing the Andes is

00:03:10.510 --> 00:03:12.350
far from straightforward.

00:03:12.920 --> 00:03:16.049
The peaks can reach 7,000 metres,

00:03:16.290 --> 00:03:19.010
which is far higher than the relatively

00:03:19.030 --> 00:03:21.480
modest military aircraft could manage.

00:03:22.190 --> 00:03:25.540
So a plane has to find a mountain

00:03:25.560 --> 00:03:28.210
pass, a gap in the mountains

00:03:28.330 --> 00:03:29.900
low enough to fly through.

00:03:31.280 --> 00:03:33.700
The previous day, on the 12th of

00:03:33.716 --> 00:03:37.070
October, the plane made its first

00:03:37.090 --> 00:03:40.239
attempt, but was turned back by

00:03:40.270 --> 00:03:42.320
bad weather over the mountains.

00:03:43.090 --> 00:03:45.079
The passengers spent the night

00:03:45.180 --> 00:03:47.549
in Mendoza, in Argentina, on

00:03:47.550 --> 00:03:49.400
the eastern side of the Andes.

00:03:50.719 --> 00:03:53.619
The next day the pilots tried again.

00:03:54.630 --> 00:03:57.059
The weather still wasn't ideal,

00:03:57.420 --> 00:03:59.609
but they decided to press on.

00:04:00.570 --> 00:04:03.010
It would prove to be a fatal decision.

00:04:04.290 --> 00:04:06.260
As the plane entered the

00:04:06.260 --> 00:04:08.660
mountains, visibility dropped.

00:04:09.219 --> 00:04:11.129
Cloud cover was thick.

00:04:11.700 --> 00:04:14.019
The pilots, who were navigating

00:04:14.270 --> 00:04:16.709
largely by calculation rather

00:04:16.709 --> 00:04:18.950
than sight, made a mistake.

00:04:20.120 --> 00:04:22.430
They believed they had already passed

00:04:22.440 --> 00:04:25.520
through the mountain pass and began

00:04:25.550 --> 00:04:27.919
their descent towards Santiago.

00:04:29.000 --> 00:04:30.879
Except…they hadn't.

00:04:32.139 --> 00:04:35.240
Instead of descending into the Chilean

00:04:35.240 --> 00:04:37.690
valleys on the other side, they were

00:04:37.690 --> 00:04:40.349
heading straight towards a mountain.

00:04:41.800 --> 00:04:44.820
When the clouds cleared and they

00:04:44.830 --> 00:04:47.519
realised their mistake, it was too late.

00:04:48.670 --> 00:04:50.849
A message crackled through the cabin:

00:04:51.559 --> 00:04:54.420
"Gentlemen, put on your seatbelts because

00:04:54.420 --> 00:04:57.110
the plane is going to dance for a while."

00:04:58.190 --> 00:05:00.950
The passengers did as they were told.

00:05:01.580 --> 00:05:03.800
Some of them were nervous, but

00:05:04.090 --> 00:05:06.239
none of them understood the full

00:05:06.250 --> 00:05:08.290
horror of what was about to happen.

00:05:09.359 --> 00:05:10.650
The turbulence began.

00:05:11.289 --> 00:05:12.920
Then came the mountain.

00:05:14.140 --> 00:05:17.010
The plane clipped a ridge at more

00:05:17.010 --> 00:05:19.229
than 300 kilometres per hour.

00:05:19.800 --> 00:05:22.990
The impact ripped the fuselage in half.

00:05:23.610 --> 00:05:25.259
The rear section, and

00:05:25.299 --> 00:05:27.240
everyone in it, was gone.

00:05:28.100 --> 00:05:30.860
The front section, meanwhile, became a

00:05:30.870 --> 00:05:33.960
metal toboggan, hurtling down a steep,

00:05:34.310 --> 00:05:37.599
snow-covered slope at terrifying speed.

00:05:39.110 --> 00:05:41.080
In what would later seem like one

00:05:41.080 --> 00:05:43.940
of several extraordinary strokes of

00:05:43.950 --> 00:05:47.019
luck, it didn't hit a single rock.

00:05:47.869 --> 00:05:50.030
Eventually, it came to rest

00:05:50.330 --> 00:05:52.719
thanks to a natural bank of snow.

00:05:53.883 --> 00:05:55.783
Twelve people had died

00:05:55.923 --> 00:05:57.153
in the crash itself.

00:05:57.523 --> 00:06:00.143
Five more died during that first

00:06:00.143 --> 00:06:02.193
night, as the temperature dropped

00:06:02.363 --> 00:06:05.303
to minus 30 degrees Celsius.

00:06:06.833 --> 00:06:09.103
When the survivors took stock

00:06:09.113 --> 00:06:10.773
of their situation the next

00:06:10.773 --> 00:06:12.973
morning, there were 27 of them.

00:06:13.873 --> 00:06:16.923
They were at around 3,600

00:06:16.963 --> 00:06:18.583
metres above sea level.

00:06:19.103 --> 00:06:21.243
They were wearing summer clothes.

00:06:21.503 --> 00:06:22.553
They had no food.

00:06:22.993 --> 00:06:24.623
They had no means of communication.

00:06:25.053 --> 00:06:27.383
And they had absolutely

00:06:27.653 --> 00:06:29.563
no idea where they were.

00:06:30.853 --> 00:06:33.893
Now, in the immediate aftermath of any

00:06:33.893 --> 00:06:36.393
disaster, there are two types of people.

00:06:36.963 --> 00:06:39.583
There are those who freeze, who are so

00:06:39.653 --> 00:06:42.533
overwhelmed by the scale of what has

00:06:42.533 --> 00:06:44.963
happened that they simply cannot function.

00:06:46.123 --> 00:06:48.023
And there are those who

00:06:48.023 --> 00:06:49.663
start solving problems.

00:06:51.003 --> 00:06:53.363
Among the survivors of the Andes

00:06:53.393 --> 00:06:55.743
crash, there were, fortunately,

00:06:56.053 --> 00:06:57.973
enough of the second type.

00:06:59.023 --> 00:07:02.003
The most immediate problem was the cold.

00:07:02.863 --> 00:07:04.973
Without shelter, a night at

00:07:04.983 --> 00:07:06.893
minus 30 would kill them.

00:07:07.743 --> 00:07:11.223
So they set to work using the wreckage of

00:07:11.223 --> 00:07:14.143
the plane itself, blocking the open end

00:07:14.143 --> 00:07:17.213
of the fuselage with suitcases, seats,

00:07:17.443 --> 00:07:20.313
and pieces of metal to keep out the wind.

00:07:20.983 --> 00:07:24.333
It was crude, basic, but it worked.

00:07:25.703 --> 00:07:27.323
There were a few medical

00:07:27.333 --> 00:07:29.243
students among the survivors.

00:07:29.543 --> 00:07:32.293
Roberto Canessa and Gustavo Zerbino

00:07:32.303 --> 00:07:35.083
in particular had only completed one

00:07:35.093 --> 00:07:37.523
year of their studies, which meant they

00:07:37.523 --> 00:07:39.823
knew just enough medicine to be useful,

00:07:40.163 --> 00:07:42.263
but not so much that they could fully

00:07:42.263 --> 00:07:44.913
appreciate just how grim things were.

00:07:45.933 --> 00:07:47.573
Perhaps that was a blessing.

00:07:48.413 --> 00:07:51.233
They set broken bones using pieces of

00:07:51.233 --> 00:07:53.793
the wreckage as splints, treated head

00:07:53.793 --> 00:07:56.363
wounds, and did what they could for

00:07:56.363 --> 00:07:58.933
those with internal injuries, all with

00:07:58.933 --> 00:08:01.513
no anaesthetic or proper equipment.

00:08:02.923 --> 00:08:04.353
Another pressing problem

00:08:04.703 --> 00:08:06.443
was the lack of water.

00:08:07.143 --> 00:08:10.123
Yes, they were surrounded by snow, but,

00:08:10.483 --> 00:08:13.393
perhaps counterintuitively, eating snow

00:08:13.553 --> 00:08:16.503
to stay hydrated is a terrible idea.

00:08:17.323 --> 00:08:19.603
To turn snow into water,

00:08:19.913 --> 00:08:22.463
your body has to heat it up.

00:08:22.943 --> 00:08:25.153
And this requires massive amount

00:08:25.343 --> 00:08:28.103
of internal energy, energy that the

00:08:28.103 --> 00:08:30.583
group needed to simply stay alive.

00:08:31.923 --> 00:08:34.383
Fortunately, one of the boys

00:08:34.673 --> 00:08:37.153
fashioned a sort of solar-powered

00:08:37.173 --> 00:08:39.403
water collector, which was just a

00:08:39.403 --> 00:08:42.223
sheet of metal heated by the rays of

00:08:42.223 --> 00:08:44.553
the sun, which could melt the snow.

00:08:45.503 --> 00:08:47.013
There was still the problem of what

00:08:47.013 --> 00:08:49.563
to eat, but we’ll come to that later.

00:08:50.783 --> 00:08:54.683
On a more high-tech note, fortunately one

00:08:54.683 --> 00:08:56.993
of the group was an engineering student.

00:08:57.523 --> 00:09:00.273
He found a small radio among the

00:09:00.273 --> 00:09:02.723
wreckage and, with some considerable

00:09:02.763 --> 00:09:05.053
ingenuity, managed to build

00:09:05.083 --> 00:09:07.273
an antenna and get it working.

00:09:07.953 --> 00:09:10.483
It could receive broadcasts, but

00:09:10.733 --> 00:09:12.963
it couldn’t transmit anything back.

00:09:13.723 --> 00:09:16.113
Still, it was a source of hope.

00:09:16.443 --> 00:09:18.763
They could hear the outside world.

00:09:19.433 --> 00:09:21.383
And initially, the outside

00:09:21.383 --> 00:09:23.603
world was looking for them.

00:09:24.393 --> 00:09:28.463
But the Andes are vast, and the

00:09:28.463 --> 00:09:30.693
authorities didn’t know exactly

00:09:30.803 --> 00:09:32.463
where the plane had crashed.

00:09:33.443 --> 00:09:35.203
Several times, the group

00:09:35.223 --> 00:09:37.260
saw planes flying overhead.

00:09:37.849 --> 00:09:39.939
They jumped up, screaming for

00:09:39.939 --> 00:09:42.449
help, but collapsed to the

00:09:42.449 --> 00:09:44.889
floor as the planes flew by.

00:09:46.059 --> 00:09:48.599
The search party simply couldn’t see them;

00:09:49.189 --> 00:09:52.399
the wreckage was white against white snow.

00:09:53.919 --> 00:09:57.689
Then, on day ten, the radio operator

00:09:57.999 --> 00:10:00.809
Roy Harley picked up a news bulletin

00:10:01.079 --> 00:10:03.433
that made the group’s  hearts sink.

00:10:04.443 --> 00:10:06.913
The search had been called off.

00:10:07.653 --> 00:10:10.413
The authorities had declared them dead.

00:10:11.583 --> 00:10:12.683
Nobody was coming.

00:10:14.046 --> 00:10:16.786
There was an immediate collapse of spirit.

00:10:17.206 --> 00:10:20.266
The news hit them like a second crash.

00:10:21.186 --> 00:10:24.296
But then, strangely, something shifted.

00:10:25.106 --> 00:10:27.796
This knowledge that they were on

00:10:27.796 --> 00:10:30.196
their own now forced a kind of

00:10:30.466 --> 00:10:33.206
clarity, a newfound sense of urgency.

00:10:33.856 --> 00:10:35.146
They stopped waiting.

00:10:35.666 --> 00:10:36.746
They started planning.

00:10:37.386 --> 00:10:40.606
But planning requires energy, and

00:10:40.626 --> 00:10:43.746
energy requires food, and food was

00:10:43.746 --> 00:10:45.486
the one thing they did not have.

00:10:46.656 --> 00:10:50.526
In those first ten days, 27 people,

00:10:50.816 --> 00:10:53.816
grown men, big rugby players, they

00:10:53.826 --> 00:10:57.086
had shared one small tin of preserved

00:10:57.096 --> 00:10:59.846
food and two squares of chocolate.

00:11:00.726 --> 00:11:01.386
That was it.

00:11:02.326 --> 00:11:04.006
After a week and a half of

00:11:04.006 --> 00:11:06.176
starvation, their bodies were

00:11:06.186 --> 00:11:08.256
beginning to consume themselves.

00:11:09.386 --> 00:11:13.006
And so, around day ten, they arrived

00:11:13.216 --> 00:11:15.746
at the most agonising decision any

00:11:15.746 --> 00:11:18.166
of them had ever faced, or that

00:11:18.176 --> 00:11:19.926
most people would ever be asked

00:11:19.936 --> 00:11:21.936
to face in their entire lives.

00:11:23.106 --> 00:11:24.216
There was food.

00:11:24.806 --> 00:11:26.656
The bodies of their dead companions

00:11:26.996 --> 00:11:29.556
were outside, preserved by the snow.

00:11:30.896 --> 00:11:32.566
Nobody wanted to say it.

00:11:33.116 --> 00:11:34.966
But eventually, someone did.

00:11:36.006 --> 00:11:38.876
Now, to reiterate, these were not

00:11:39.126 --> 00:11:41.576
hardened mountaineers or soldiers.

00:11:41.836 --> 00:11:44.796
They were twenty-year-olds, boys really,

00:11:45.076 --> 00:11:47.196
from comfortable homes in Montevideo.

00:11:48.116 --> 00:11:51.036
Many of them were devout Catholics for

00:11:51.036 --> 00:11:53.506
whom this decision carried not only

00:11:53.576 --> 00:11:56.556
physical but profound spiritual weight.

00:11:57.906 --> 00:12:00.356
At first, not everyone could

00:12:00.356 --> 00:12:01.446
bring themselves to do it.

00:12:02.406 --> 00:12:04.586
Some refused, mostly

00:12:04.586 --> 00:12:05.946
for religious reasons.

00:12:06.666 --> 00:12:09.896
But in the end, everyone ate.

00:12:10.606 --> 00:12:12.796
They made a solemn pact: if

00:12:12.816 --> 00:12:14.926
any of them died, they would

00:12:14.996 --> 00:12:17.156
offer their body to the others.

00:12:17.866 --> 00:12:19.516
It was a pact of survival.

00:12:20.146 --> 00:12:23.096
And it gave them, as one survivor

00:12:23.446 --> 00:12:25.436
would later put it, the right

00:12:25.666 --> 00:12:27.176
to fight for their lives.

00:12:28.426 --> 00:12:30.696
Some later explained that they

00:12:30.696 --> 00:12:32.906
thought of it not as an act of

00:12:32.976 --> 00:12:35.966
violence, but as an act of communion,

00:12:36.196 --> 00:12:38.306
that the dead were giving their

00:12:38.306 --> 00:12:41.156
bodies so the living might survive.

00:12:42.246 --> 00:12:45.436
And they worked carefully, respectfully.

00:12:45.816 --> 00:12:47.546
They avoided looking at faces.

00:12:47.826 --> 00:12:50.176
The women’s bodies were left untouched.

00:12:52.046 --> 00:12:53.866
I should pause here for a moment,

00:12:53.926 --> 00:12:56.356
because this story has some

00:12:56.376 --> 00:12:59.106
remarkable individuals, and one

00:12:59.156 --> 00:13:01.316
in particular deserves a mention.

00:13:02.156 --> 00:13:04.286
Fernando Parrado, otherwise known

00:13:04.286 --> 00:13:07.216
simply as Nando, suffered a severe

00:13:07.216 --> 00:13:09.346
blow to the head in the crash.

00:13:09.806 --> 00:13:11.916
His companions initially

00:13:11.926 --> 00:13:13.096
thought he was dead.

00:13:13.726 --> 00:13:15.816
He lay unconscious for three

00:13:15.816 --> 00:13:18.236
days before waking from a coma.

00:13:19.236 --> 00:13:21.476
When he came round, he learned that

00:13:21.486 --> 00:13:23.706
his mother, who had also been on

00:13:23.706 --> 00:13:25.896
the plane, had died in the crash.

00:13:26.616 --> 00:13:29.306
And then, days later, he watched his

00:13:29.316 --> 00:13:31.706
younger sister die of her injuries.

00:13:32.416 --> 00:13:34.726
The grief he must have felt is

00:13:34.756 --> 00:13:37.006
almost impossible to imagine.

00:13:37.966 --> 00:13:41.216
And yet Nando Parrado became, in the weeks

00:13:41.216 --> 00:13:43.936
that followed, one of the driving forces

00:13:44.006 --> 00:13:46.696
of the survivors' determination to escape.

00:13:47.576 --> 00:13:50.096
But before that escape became

00:13:50.096 --> 00:13:52.736
possible, there was another catastrophe

00:13:54.336 --> 00:13:57.926
On 29 October, seventeen days after

00:13:57.926 --> 00:14:00.516
the crash, there was a huge avalanche.

00:14:00.806 --> 00:14:03.566
A wall of snow poured down the

00:14:03.566 --> 00:14:06.006
mountainside and into the fuselage.

00:14:06.556 --> 00:14:08.516
Eight more people were killed.

00:14:09.126 --> 00:14:11.126
The survivors were buried under

00:14:11.126 --> 00:14:14.146
metres of snow for three days.

00:14:14.686 --> 00:14:18.116
Three days in total darkness, not knowing

00:14:18.116 --> 00:14:20.166
if there was enough air, not knowing if

00:14:20.166 --> 00:14:22.566
another avalanche was coming, not knowing

00:14:22.786 --> 00:14:25.246
if they would ever dig their way out.

00:14:26.196 --> 00:14:27.816
Eventually, they did.

00:14:28.996 --> 00:14:30.446
There were now fewer

00:14:30.476 --> 00:14:32.346
than twenty of them left.

00:14:33.046 --> 00:14:35.746
And the experience had made one

00:14:35.746 --> 00:14:39.406
thing absolutely clear: staying in

00:14:39.406 --> 00:14:41.816
that place meant waiting for death.

00:14:42.566 --> 00:14:43.886
If they were going to

00:14:43.886 --> 00:14:46.116
survive, they had to get out.

00:14:46.786 --> 00:14:47.436
On their own.

00:14:48.036 --> 00:14:49.656
Their survival depended

00:14:49.696 --> 00:14:52.126
on them, and them alone.

00:14:53.816 --> 00:14:56.196
Their first practical challenge was

00:14:56.196 --> 00:14:58.516
finding the tail section of the plane,

00:14:58.726 --> 00:15:01.446
which had separated from the fuselage

00:15:01.456 --> 00:15:03.626
during the crash and come to rest

00:15:03.636 --> 00:15:05.286
somewhere further down the slope.

00:15:06.116 --> 00:15:09.746
In the tail were their suitcases, clothes,

00:15:09.976 --> 00:15:12.546
useful objects, and possibly food.

00:15:13.136 --> 00:15:15.486
And, crucially, the aircraft's

00:15:15.486 --> 00:15:18.436
batteries, which they could use to fix

00:15:18.436 --> 00:15:20.786
the plane’s transmitter and allow them

00:15:20.946 --> 00:15:23.526
to send messages to the outside world.

00:15:24.816 --> 00:15:26.226
After several days of

00:15:26.226 --> 00:15:27.736
searching, they found it.

00:15:28.526 --> 00:15:31.176
And while there was indeed clothing and

00:15:31.186 --> 00:15:33.956
supplies, the attempt to use the batteries

00:15:34.166 --> 00:15:36.836
to fix the transmitter ultimately failed.

00:15:37.466 --> 00:15:39.316
There would be no calling for help.

00:15:40.246 --> 00:15:43.256
But in the tail they found

00:15:43.296 --> 00:15:45.476
something else: an insulating

00:15:45.486 --> 00:15:48.146
material, a kind of foam padding.

00:15:49.366 --> 00:15:53.016
And with it, they had a flash of genius.

00:15:54.646 --> 00:15:57.816
They fashioned it into a sleeping bag,

00:15:58.146 --> 00:16:01.876
crude and improvised, but warm enough to

00:16:01.876 --> 00:16:05.106
allow a person to spend a night outside

00:16:05.106 --> 00:16:07.836
the fuselage in sub-zero temperatures.

00:16:08.746 --> 00:16:10.506
This changed everything.

00:16:10.906 --> 00:16:12.876
Because now they could walk, they

00:16:12.876 --> 00:16:14.916
could travel for longer than a day,

00:16:15.096 --> 00:16:17.126
without having to return to the

00:16:17.126 --> 00:16:19.126
safety of the plane to escape the

00:16:19.126 --> 00:16:21.336
cruel freezing night of the Andes.

00:16:22.306 --> 00:16:25.556
The plan was this: three of the

00:16:25.556 --> 00:16:28.596
fittest survivors would set out on

00:16:28.596 --> 00:16:31.356
an expedition westward, hoping to

00:16:31.386 --> 00:16:33.836
cross the mountains and reach Chile.

00:16:34.886 --> 00:16:37.236
Before they could do that, they needed

00:16:37.236 --> 00:16:39.386
to know what they were walking into.

00:16:40.126 --> 00:16:42.466
So first, they climbed.

00:16:42.886 --> 00:16:43.686
Straight up.

00:16:44.156 --> 00:16:47.886
A ridge at over 4,500 metres that

00:16:47.886 --> 00:16:49.806
took them three days to summit.

00:16:50.946 --> 00:16:53.496
When they reached the top, they looked

00:16:53.526 --> 00:16:56.726
west, hoping to see green valleys,

00:16:56.986 --> 00:16:59.976
trees, the brown of lower earth.

00:17:00.136 --> 00:17:02.466
Something alive, something that

00:17:02.496 --> 00:17:04.896
indicated that there was a way out.

00:17:05.816 --> 00:17:08.306
What they saw was more mountains.

00:17:08.856 --> 00:17:11.506
Nothing but white in every direction

00:17:11.996 --> 00:17:13.606
as far as the eye could see.

00:17:14.806 --> 00:17:17.529
The three men, Roberto Canessa,

00:17:17.695 --> 00:17:19.386
Nando Parrado, and Antonio

00:17:19.386 --> 00:17:22.206
Vizintín, their hearts sank.

00:17:23.206 --> 00:17:26.046
Canessa's first instinct was to turn back.

00:17:26.646 --> 00:17:28.586
It seemed hopeless.

00:17:29.186 --> 00:17:31.136
But Nando Parrado refused.

00:17:31.576 --> 00:17:32.636
He had lost his mother

00:17:32.976 --> 00:17:34.226
and his younger sister.

00:17:34.556 --> 00:17:37.846
He had woken from a coma against all odds.

00:17:38.386 --> 00:17:41.456
And he had decided, with absolute

00:17:41.456 --> 00:17:44.656
clarity, that he would rather die trying

00:17:44.666 --> 00:17:47.446
to get out than die waiting to be found.

00:17:48.396 --> 00:17:50.656
"If I have to die," he is reported to

00:17:50.656 --> 00:17:52.926
have said, "I want to die walking."

00:17:54.292 --> 00:17:56.506
Canessa agreed to continue.

00:17:57.238 --> 00:18:00.016
Vizintín turned back, sliding down the

00:18:00.016 --> 00:18:03.376
mountain on an improvised suitcase-sled to

00:18:03.376 --> 00:18:05.256
let the others know what they had found.

00:18:06.516 --> 00:18:08.716
And so Nando Parrado and

00:18:08.716 --> 00:18:10.476
Roberto Cannessa walked.

00:18:11.146 --> 00:18:12.536
Ten days in total.

00:18:12.936 --> 00:18:16.186
Through snow and wind and altitude and

00:18:16.196 --> 00:18:19.096
exhaustion, in conditions that should

00:18:19.106 --> 00:18:21.226
have been beyond the limits of human

00:18:21.226 --> 00:18:23.936
endurance, especially given that they

00:18:23.936 --> 00:18:26.016
were two months into this ordeal.

00:18:27.566 --> 00:18:30.056
Slowly, over the days that followed,

00:18:30.306 --> 00:18:32.576
the landscape began to change.

00:18:33.096 --> 00:18:34.696
The snow thinned.

00:18:35.316 --> 00:18:36.066
There was green.

00:18:36.576 --> 00:18:38.366
There were signs that the world

00:18:38.396 --> 00:18:40.686
below, the living world, was

00:18:40.686 --> 00:18:42.986
not entirely out of reach.

00:18:44.506 --> 00:18:47.066
And then, on the ninth day of

00:18:47.066 --> 00:18:48.896
walking, they found a river.

00:18:49.686 --> 00:18:50.446
They followed it.

00:18:51.096 --> 00:18:53.066
And on the other side of the

00:18:53.066 --> 00:18:56.116
river, they saw a man on horseback.

00:18:57.196 --> 00:18:59.126
The river was too loud and

00:18:59.126 --> 00:19:00.516
too wide to shout across.

00:19:00.876 --> 00:19:03.956
So Nando tore off a piece of paper,

00:19:04.396 --> 00:19:06.696
wrote a message explaining who they

00:19:06.696 --> 00:19:09.466
were and what had happened, tied it to

00:19:09.466 --> 00:19:12.516
a stone, and threw it to the other bank.

00:19:13.796 --> 00:19:15.836
The man was a Chilean herdsman.

00:19:16.626 --> 00:19:17.796
He picked up the message.

00:19:18.106 --> 00:19:19.846
And he went to find help.

00:19:20.656 --> 00:19:23.786
Back at the crash site, those who had

00:19:23.786 --> 00:19:26.136
stayed behind heard the news on the

00:19:26.136 --> 00:19:30.076
radio: Parrado and Canessa had been found.

00:19:30.746 --> 00:19:33.096
Help was on the way.

00:19:33.756 --> 00:19:36.236
The rescue operation moved quickly.

00:19:36.866 --> 00:19:39.066
Parrado guided the helicopters

00:19:39.226 --> 00:19:41.356
back to the crash site himself.

00:19:42.026 --> 00:19:45.546
The terrain was so steep and the altitude

00:19:45.566 --> 00:19:47.706
so high that the helicopters could

00:19:47.736 --> 00:19:50.236
only carry a few survivors at a time.

00:19:50.756 --> 00:19:53.256
It took two days to get everyone out.

00:19:54.226 --> 00:19:56.756
The families of the survivors, who had

00:19:56.756 --> 00:20:00.206
spent 72 days grieving people they had

00:20:00.206 --> 00:20:02.616
been told were dead, learned that their

00:20:02.616 --> 00:20:06.366
sons and brothers and fathers were alive.

00:20:07.456 --> 00:20:09.916
16 people had survived.

00:20:10.946 --> 00:20:14.376
Now, when the story became public, when

00:20:14.376 --> 00:20:17.276
it became clear how the survivors had

00:20:17.276 --> 00:20:19.846
kept themselves alive, the reaction

00:20:19.866 --> 00:20:22.566
was complicated, to say the least.

00:20:22.926 --> 00:20:25.506
There was enormous relief, obvious

00:20:25.686 --> 00:20:28.476
joy, especially from their loved ones.

00:20:29.146 --> 00:20:31.406
But there was also shock,

00:20:32.036 --> 00:20:34.306
and for some, condemnation.

00:20:35.586 --> 00:20:37.836
Some newspapers used the word

00:20:38.126 --> 00:20:40.476
“cannibalism” in large headlines.

00:20:41.146 --> 00:20:43.016
Others called it a miracle.

00:20:43.996 --> 00:20:46.316
The Catholic Church, to the

00:20:46.316 --> 00:20:48.656
surprise of many, came out quickly

00:20:48.656 --> 00:20:50.706
in support of the survivors.

00:20:51.376 --> 00:20:54.096
The bishop of Montevideo stated

00:20:54.196 --> 00:20:56.606
clearly that what they had done was

00:20:56.616 --> 00:20:59.626
morally justified, that the Christian

00:20:59.626 --> 00:21:02.796
tradition recognises the sanctity of

00:21:02.796 --> 00:21:06.006
life, and that the dead had been used

00:21:06.146 --> 00:21:09.462
to save the living, not desecrated.

00:21:10.226 --> 00:21:12.086
Most people, over time,

00:21:12.466 --> 00:21:14.246
came to the same conclusion.

00:21:15.186 --> 00:21:18.296
And two of the protagonists of

00:21:18.296 --> 00:21:21.576
this story, its heroes, even, Nando

00:21:21.576 --> 00:21:23.956
Parrado and Roberto Canessa, they

00:21:23.956 --> 00:21:26.696
would go on to live remarkable lives.

00:21:27.396 --> 00:21:29.246
Canessa finished his medical

00:21:29.246 --> 00:21:31.566
studies and became a distinguished

00:21:31.796 --> 00:21:33.436
paediatric cardiologist.

00:21:33.806 --> 00:21:36.366
Parrado became a successful entrepreneur.

00:21:36.976 --> 00:21:39.236
Both have spoken and written

00:21:39.246 --> 00:21:41.576
extensively about their experience.

00:21:42.466 --> 00:21:45.506
Carlos Páez, one of the survivors whose

00:21:45.506 --> 00:21:47.726
accounts were drawn on in preparing

00:21:47.726 --> 00:21:50.186
this episode, has perhaps put the

00:21:50.206 --> 00:21:53.526
philosophy of those 72 days most simply.

00:21:54.496 --> 00:21:56.636
According to him, in the beginning

00:21:57.026 --> 00:21:58.446
they did what was necessary.

00:21:59.066 --> 00:22:01.296
Then they did what was possible.

00:22:01.996 --> 00:22:03.236
And in the end, they

00:22:03.236 --> 00:22:04.536
achieved the impossible.

00:22:05.556 --> 00:22:08.306
It is, undoubtedly, one of the greatest

00:22:08.316 --> 00:22:11.046
stories of survival ever to be told.

00:22:12.526 --> 00:22:15.466
OK then, that is it for today's episode on

00:22:15.466 --> 00:22:19.236
the Andes disaster of 1972, or, depending

00:22:19.236 --> 00:22:21.526
on how you look at it, the Andes miracle.

00:22:22.096 --> 00:22:23.956
Again, a huge thank you and

00:22:23.986 --> 00:22:25.916
congratulations to Javier

00:22:26.076 --> 00:22:27.776
from the Radio ELE podcast.

00:22:28.036 --> 00:22:30.106
I get emails all the time from people

00:22:30.246 --> 00:22:31.556
who say that they are thinking of

00:22:31.556 --> 00:22:33.586
doing a similar thing, and that they

00:22:33.586 --> 00:22:35.476
would like advice, but this is the

00:22:35.486 --> 00:22:37.546
first time I’ve ever got one from

00:22:37.546 --> 00:22:39.736
someone who has actually just started.

00:22:40.046 --> 00:22:42.856
So, Javier, well done, and thank you

00:22:42.866 --> 00:22:45.186
for sharing this story, I hope it might

00:22:45.186 --> 00:22:47.746
provide inspiration to someone out there.

00:22:48.476 --> 00:22:50.276
You’ve been listening to English Learning

00:22:50.276 --> 00:22:53.086
for Curious Minds, by Leonardo English.

00:22:53.396 --> 00:22:56.056
I'm Alastair Budge, you stay safe, and

00:22:56.056 --> 00:22:58.286
I'll catch you in the next episode.

